class ApiVersioningControls(_messages.Message):
  r"""Used to control ApiVersion tooling. Contains configuration that
  influences top-level tooling behavior and policy validation.

  Enums:
    DefaultGroupsValueValuesEnum: The grouping strategy for API interfaces. A
      "group" is a collection of API interfaces that are versioned together as
      a single unit. When any interface within a group receives an update that
      requires a new API version, all other interfaces within that same group
      also receive a new API version identifier. This ensures that all
      interfaces in a group share the same set of version identifiers. NOTE:
      Explicit grouping is planned to be introduced. Once introduced, this
      setting will determine the grouping strategy for interfaces that are not
      explicitly assigned to a group.

  Fields:
    defaultConstraints: Top level default constraints are applied to new API
      versions that do not have explicit constraints defined and are not
      subject to any constraint overrides as well. A new API version is a
      version for which a yaml config file does not already exist. For
      example, if an API version is created without specifying any
      constraints, the default constraints will be applied to it.
      api_versioning: controls: default_constraints: - visibility:
      GOOGLE_INTERNAL In this case, all new API versions that do not have
      explicit constraints will be marked as internal. This is useful to
      prevent accidental exposure of a new API version to the public. Multiple
      default_constraints are combined using any_true logic.
    defaultGroups: The grouping strategy for API interfaces. A "group" is a
      collection of API interfaces that are versioned together as a single
      unit. When any interface within a group receives an update that requires
      a new API version, all other interfaces within that same group also
      receive a new API version identifier. This ensures that all interfaces
      in a group share the same set of version identifiers. NOTE: Explicit
      grouping is planned to be introduced. Once introduced, this setting will
      determine the grouping strategy for interfaces that are not explicitly
      assigned to a group.
    overrides: Overrides are used to assign the properties of ApiVersion in
      bulk. The most common case is where, for all API interfaces which have
      the same API version value, the parents and constraints must snap to the
      same configuration. The overrides have stronger priority than
      configuration found in individual ApiVersion yaml files. For clarity,
      running `api_update` BUILD rule will also modify the properties those
      yaml files with the configuration determined by overrides. The following
      example makes all API of two api_version values internal.
      api_versioning: controls: overrides: - matches: - api_version:
      2026-04-01-preview - api_version: 2026-07-01-preview constraints: -
      visibility: GOOGLE_INTERNAL All matching overrides are applied, and the
      overrides are applied in order. For repeated fields, if there are
      multiple matches, only the last match with one or more item in that
      repeated field is applied.
    protoPackages: Proto packages subject to Interface Based Versioning (IBV).
      If specified, only interfaces in these packages are able to have IBV
      version identifiers defined. In all other packages the
      `api_versioning.restriction` values are ignored, and corresponding
      `api_versioning.api_versions[]` in service data is disallowed. If not
      specified, all packages are subject to IBV.
  """

  class DefaultGroupsValueValuesEnum(_messages.Enum):
    r"""The grouping strategy for API interfaces. A "group" is a collection of
    API interfaces that are versioned together as a single unit. When any
    interface within a group receives an update that requires a new API
    version, all other interfaces within that same group also receive a new
    API version identifier. This ensures that all interfaces in a group share
    the same set of version identifiers. NOTE: Explicit grouping is planned to
    be introduced. Once introduced, this setting will determine the grouping
    strategy for interfaces that are not explicitly assigned to a group.

    Values:
      DEFAULT_GROUPS_UNSPECIFIED: Uses the `DEFAULT_GROUPS_PER_INTERFACE`
        strategy if unspecified.
      DEFAULT_GROUPS_PER_PACKAGE: All interfaces in a proto package acquire
        new version identifiers altogether. Interfaces will acquire versions
        that are technically not necessary, but the assumption you may send
        the same set of `X-Goog-Api-Version` values for any request simplifies
        the usage greatly. For example, consider a package with `Instances`,
        `Reservations`, and `Disks` services. Timeline of changes: 1.
        `Instances` gains a field, triggering version `2025-06-01`. 2.
        `Reservations` gains a field, triggering version `2025-07-01`. 3.
        `Disks` gains a field, triggering version `2025-12-01`. Under
        `DEFAULT_GROUPS_PER_PACKAGE`, every service gets every version, even
        if they didn't change: Version 2025-06-01: Instances @2025-06-01
        (changed) Reservations @2025-06-01 (no change, but versioned up) Disks
        @2025-06-01 (no change, but versioned up) Version 2025-07-01:
        Instances @2025-07-01 (no change, but versioned up) Reservations
        @2025-07-01 (changed) Disks @2025-07-01 (no change, but versioned up)
        Version 2025-12-01: Instances @2025-12-01 (no change, but versioned
        up) Reservations @2025-12-01 (no change, but versioned up) Disks
        @2025-12-01 (changed) Valid access URLs for `2025-12-01` would show
        all services at that version: .../instances?api_version=2025-12-01
        .../reservations?api_version=2025-12-01
        .../disks?api_version=2025-12-01
      DEFAULT_GROUPS_PER_INTERFACE: Each interface will acquire new version
        identifiers individually. This is recommended for services which
        comprise several functionally distinct business domains, or which
        contain multiple development teams working independently. This is the
        default behavior. Using the same example as above: Timeline of
        changes: 1. `Instances` gains a field, triggering version
        `2025-06-01`. 2. `Reservations` gains a field, triggering version
        `2025-07-01`. 3. `Disks` gains a field, triggering version
        `2025-12-01`. Under `DEFAULT_GROUPS_PER_INTERFACE`, only the changed
        service (and any others that might have changed) gets the new version.
        Others stay at their previous version. Version 2025-06-01: Instances
        @2025-06-01 (changed) Reservations @2025-01-01 (previous stable) Disks
        @2025-01-01 (previous stable) Version 2025-07-01: Instances
        @2025-06-01 (from previous release) Reservations @2025-07-01 (changed)
        Disks @2025-01-01 (previous stable) Version 2025-12-01: Instances
        @2025-06-01 (from previous release) Reservations @2025-07-01 (from
        previous release) Disks @2025-12-01 (changed) Valid access URLs for
        the `2025-12-01` "release" (which acts as a high water mark) would mix
        versions: .../instances?api_version=2025-06-01
        .../reservations?api_version=2025-07-01
        .../disks?api_version=2025-12-01
    """
    DEFAULT_GROUPS_UNSPECIFIED = 0
    DEFAULT_GROUPS_PER_PACKAGE = 1
    DEFAULT_GROUPS_PER_INTERFACE = 2

  defaultConstraints = _messages.MessageField('ApiVersionConstraint', 1, repeated=True)
  defaultGroups = _messages.EnumField('DefaultGroupsValueValuesEnum', 2)
  overrides = _messages.MessageField('ApiVersionOverride', 3, repeated=True)
  protoPackages = _messages.StringField(4, repeated=True)


class ApiVersioningOptions(_messages.Message):
  r"""Additional options that can be specified by the service producers to
  handle special cases for e.g. retaining map key in case of null value.

  Fields:
    clientOverrides: Rules to override the API version resolution for specific
      client projects. This is typically used to gracefully handle clients
      sending known invalid version strings through any mechanism (e.g., HTTP
      headers, side-channel).
    retainKeyWhenNullValueMap: boolean option to determine whether to retain a
      map key in case it's corresponding value is null. This will be ignored
      if interface based versioning is not enabled.
  """

  clientOverrides = _messages.MessageField('ApiVersionClientOverrideRule', 1, repeated=True)
  retainKeyWhenNullValueMap = _messages.BooleanField(2)


class ApiVersionsRule(_messages.Message):
  r"""A versioning rule that can be applied to any proto element. This is used
  to limit which API versions the matching proto elements can be a part of.
  NOTEs: * As with other config that can be applied to several proto element
  types (e.g. `visibility`), try not to use wildcards (e.g. `"*"` or
  `"foo.v1.*"`) too much. Since the wildcards are expanded into the normalized
  service config, a wildcard like `"*"` will end up including the full proto
  closure, bloating the size of that service config. With versioning, nested
  proto elements will only be present if their parent elements are present,
  so, for example, instead of using `"library.v1.*"` to get all of the "v1"
  proto, maybe use `"library.v1.LibraryService"` to apply to the API plus all
  of its methods. * If a given proto element is referenced in the proto
  closure of multiple APIs, it must be "present" in all of those API versions.
  For example, if a `Book` message type is referenced through `LibraryService`
  version A and through `ShelfService` version B, the `Book` type must be
  available in both versions A and B.

  Fields:
    restriction: API VERSIONING RESTRICTIONS To control which api elements are
      included in which API version, and how they are configured, a pattern is
      supported which is uniquely applied to annotations of the api, services,
      methods, messages, and fields. Each annotation contains an optional
      field 'restriction' which contains a string which specifies when to
      include the associated element. The restriction is given as a comma
      separated list of version specifiers which are matched against a version
      name as passed in at build time. If any of the specifiers matches the
      version name, the element is included. TODO(user) Fill in syntax
      rules. The 'restriction' field can use the special names 'all' and
      'none' to unconditionally include or exclude an element.
    selector: Selects methods, messages, fields, enums, etc. to which this
      rule applies. Refer to selector for syntax details.
  """

  restriction = _messages.StringField(1)
  selector = _messages.StringField(2)


class ApplicationAuthorizationCheckCondition(_messages.Message):
  r"""A ApplicationAuthorizationCheckCondition object."""


class ApplicationPermissionPolicy(_messages.Message):
  r"""Role-based policies for associating application level `permissions` with
  `bindings` and `conditions`. Only for use with 2.0 `RpcSecurityPolicy`s,
  i.e. policies that contain `endpoint_policies`. This field is compatible
  with the LocalAuthorization library, see go/rpcsp-authz#rpcsp2 DO NOT USE:
  Under development, see b/327637894 for status. The following four fields
  determines how the RpcSP compiler processes the policy configuration during
  compilation time for the given ApplicationPermissionPolicy. The fields can
  only be used in policies in the Central Repo. See http://go/centralrepo-
  policy-check-and-rewrite-guide.

  Enums:
    DisableChecksValueListEntryValuesEnum:
    DisableRewritesValueListEntryValuesEnum:
    EnableChecksValueListEntryValuesEnum:
    EnableRewritesValueListEntryValuesEnum:

  Fields:
    bindings: Optional. Binds authorities to roles. An application permission
      policy with no bindings acts as a deny-all policy. The following roles
      are supported for ApplicationPermissionPolicy: * ADMIN * SYSTEM The
      following BindingConditions are supported for
      ApplicationPermissionPolicy: * user_attribution * security_realm
    conditions: Optional. Conditions that apply to all bindings.
    dataGovernanceAnnotations: Semantic attributes of the data accessed by the
      permissions in this policy. See go/dg-annotations for details.
    disableChecks: Checks that are disabled in the RpcSP compiler
    disableRewrites: Rewrites that are disabled in the RpcSP compiler
    enableChecks: Checks that are enabled in the RpcSP compiler
    enableRewrites: Rewrites that are enabled in the RpcSP compiler
    includesDataGovernanceAnnotations: User-specified variables for templated
      DG annotations. The variables are resolved through supplying
      instantiation with matching keys. Ex:
      includes_data_governance_annotations: 'method-1-annotation' WARNING:
      this field is only supported for policies in the RpcSP central repo
      (google3/configs/security/rpcsp). See go/rpcsp2-templating for usage.
    permissions: String defining a permission that is granted to a user that
      matches this policy. `permissions` is supported by the
      LocalAuthorization library for custom authorization checks, see
      go/rpcsp-authz#rpcsp2. Must follow the format of a standard IAM
      permission, see
      http://google3/google/iam/v1/rule.proto?l=48-51&rcl=268126215.
  """

  class DisableChecksValueListEntryValuesEnum(_messages.Enum):
    r"""DisableChecksValueListEntryValuesEnum enum type.

    Values:
      CHECK_UNSPECIFIED: <no description>
      CHECK_DENY_EUC_PRESENTER_ADMIN: This check complains if the
        `EUC_PRESENTER_ADMIN` role is used in a policy. Such role should not
        be used, as it allows humans to send EUCs, which prevents the server
        reaching RpcSecurityPolicy level 3.3.
      CHECK_REQUIRE_ORDER_INDEPENDENT_BINDINGS: This check ensures that
        bindings are used in a way that the ordering of the bindings isn't
        significant.
      CHECK_DENY_UNPRIVILEGED_USER_NARROW_BINDING: This check verifies that
        UNPRIVILEGED_USER does not grant access to a narrow set of users.
      CHECK_LANGUAGE_SUPPORT: This check verifies that a policy does not use
        unsupported language features. This is an internal compiler feature
        and cannot be disabled by users.
      CHECK_EXCLUDE_INSECURE_USERS_CONDITION: This check ensures that the
        `exclude_insecure_users` condition can only be used to restrict
        supported users. See go/rpcsp-levels#insecure-roles-list. This is an
        internal compiler feature and cannot be disabled by users.
      CHECK_ADMIN_ACCESS_CONTROLS: This check ensures that
        `admin_access_control` is only set on bindings for admin roles. This
        is an internal compiler feature and cannot be disabled by users.
      CHECK_CONFLICTING_GIN_LOGGING_BEHAVIORS: This check ensures that all
        *ADMIN roles (e.g. `ADMIN`, `EUC_PRESENTER_ADMIN`,
        `IMPERSONATOR_ADMIN`) for a single endpoint are using the same Gin
        logging failure behavior. Configuring two *ADMIN bindings where one
        binding is set to use fail closed Gin logging and the other is set to
        use fail open Gin logging is equivalent to configuring one binding
        that is using fail open Gin logging, and is therefore not allowed.
      CHECK_NO_HTTP_HANDLERS_FIELD: This check complains if an
        `endpoint_policy` uses the experimental `http_handlers` field.
      CHECK_NO_OAUTH_IN_BINDINGS: This check ensures `OauthCondition` is NOT
        used at the `Binding`-level, only at the `EndpointPolicy`-level.
      CHECK_CORP_GROUPS_HTTPSP_ONLY: This check limits the use of the field
        `corp_groups` to HttpSP.
      CHECK_REQUIRE_DG_ANNOTATIONS: This check requires that all `mapping` and
        `endpoint_policies` entries configure Data Governance Annotations
        (go/dga). This check is never enabled by default, and should only be
        enabled if you understand its effects.
      CHECK_CORP_GROUPS_ALLOWED_BINDINGS_ONLY: This check limits the use of
        the field `corp_groups` to only `ADMIN` and `SYSTEM` bindings.
      CHECK_NO_LOCAL_ACLS: This check prohibits use of go/localacl (RpcSP2
        `local_prod_groups` and RpcSP1 `local:groupname`). Uses of this
        feature must be approved by ProdSec Labs.
      CHECK_NO_STUBBY_APPLICATION_AUTHORIZATION: This check prevents a mapping
        or endpoint_policy from allowing [fine-grained
        authorization](http://go/rpc-security-simple#authorization). If a
        service records fine-grained authorization results, then this check
        can be safely disabled. Warning: framework-specific filtering is
        applied for this check in [PolicyChecker::Check](http://cs/function:rp
        csp::internal::PolicyChecker::Check$). Currently, only applicable to
        Stubby services.
      CHECK_HTTPSP_AUDIT_ONLY_ENABLE_OR_DISABLE: This check prevents the use
        of values except `ENABLE_LOGGING` or `DISABLE_LOGGING` for `audit` in
        `disabled_admin_access_controls` for an HttpSP policy. HttpSP Gin
        logging has a hard dependency on AAP because that is where auto
        population of the DataAccessLog based on the incoming HTTP request
        occurs. As a result, we disallow Gin logging without consultation of
        AAP for HttpSP, i.e. `ENABLE_LOGGING_LITE`.
      CHECK_NO_EXCLUDE_PEER_USER_CONDITION: This check prevents use of the
        `exclude_peer_user` and `exclude_primary_peer_user` conditions.
      CHECK_REQUIRE_RPC_SERVICES_FIELD: This check enforces the existence of
        the `rpc_services` field. Warning: framework-specific filtering is
        applied for this check in [PolicyChecker::Check](http://cs/function:rp
        csp::internal::PolicyChecker::Check$). Currently, only applicable to
        Stubby services.
      CHECK_HTTPSP_MEETING_CORP_WEB_MANDATORY_CONTROL: This check requires the
        HttpSP policy to meet the requirements of the mandatory control for
        corp web access: 1. The policy is from an EASE managed directory (e.g.
        google3/configs/security/rpcsp/teams/aclaim/managed/). 2. The policy
        allows only devices of fully trusted tier and LOAS origins. 3. Gin
        logging is enabled. 4. Privileged access is subject to AAP control. 5.
        The policy is HttpSP policy (i.e., endpoints are identified using only
        `http_handlers` or `http_paths`). 6. The policy is in PROD
        environment. This check is never enabled by default, and should only
        be enabled if you understand its effects. See go/rpcsp2-policy-
        controls#check-httpsp-meeting-corp-web-mandatory-control
      CHECK_NO_OVERLAPPING_HUMAN_PROD_BINDINGS: This check enforces that no
        more than one binding may grant any given permission to the same set
        of human prod users. If this check passes, it means that binding order
        (and the major exception documented at go/rpcsp2-binding-
        evaluation#binding-evaluation-order) cannot affect how requests from
        human prod users are evaluated. This check is intentionally very
        strict, and serves as a guardrail to encourage more readable policies.
        It may be disabled without an exemption, but we strongly recommend
        that service owners only do so if the check is failing due to a false
        positive, or if you require multiple overlapping admin bindings and
        are following the guidelines at go/rpcsp2-configuring-admin-role.
      CHECK_NO_ALL_NORMAL_USERS_IN_SYSTEM_ROLE: This check prevents the use of
        `all_normal_users` in bindings for the `SYSTEM` role. **Why this check
        exists:** The `SYSTEM` role only grants access to non-person prod
        users (such as Borg roles, see go/rpcsp2-roles#system-vs-admin-roles),
        but `all_normal_users` matches authenticated Gaia users who are *not*
        prod users. Therefore, granting the `SYSTEM` role to
        `all_normal_users` has no effect; this check prevents users from
        configuring RpcSP in this confusing way. **How to fix it:** If your
        policy fails this check, you must remove `all_normal_users` from the
        `members` field of any binding where `role` is `SYSTEM`. Replace it
        with a more specific set of users or groups that require this level of
        access.
    """
    CHECK_UNSPECIFIED = 0
    CHECK_DENY_EUC_PRESENTER_ADMIN = 1
    CHECK_REQUIRE_ORDER_INDEPENDENT_BINDINGS = 2
    CHECK_DENY_UNPRIVILEGED_USER_NARROW_BINDING = 3
    CHECK_LANGUAGE_SUPPORT = 4
    CHECK_EXCLUDE_INSECURE_USERS_CONDITION = 5
    CHECK_ADMIN_ACCESS_CONTROLS = 6
    CHECK_CONFLICTING_GIN_LOGGING_BEHAVIORS = 7
    CHECK_NO_HTTP_HANDLERS_FIELD = 8
    CHECK_NO_OAUTH_IN_BINDINGS = 9
    CHECK_CORP_GROUPS_HTTPSP_ONLY = 10
    CHECK_REQUIRE_DG_ANNOTATIONS = 11
    CHECK_CORP_GROUPS_ALLOWED_BINDINGS_ONLY = 12
    CHECK_NO_LOCAL_ACLS = 13
    CHECK_NO_STUBBY_APPLICATION_AUTHORIZATION = 14
    CHECK_HTTPSP_AUDIT_ONLY_ENABLE_OR_DISABLE = 15
    CHECK_NO_EXCLUDE_PEER_USER_CONDITION = 16
    CHECK_REQUIRE_RPC_SERVICES_FIELD = 17
    CHECK_HTTPSP_MEETING_CORP_WEB_MANDATORY_CONTROL = 18
    CHECK_NO_OVERLAPPING_HUMAN_PROD_BINDINGS = 19
    CHECK_NO_ALL_NORMAL_USERS_IN_SYSTEM_ROLE = 20

  class DisableRewritesValueListEntryValuesEnum(_messages.Enum):
    r"""DisableRewritesValueListEntryValuesEnum enum type.

    Values:
      REWRITE_UNSPECIFIED: <no description>
      REWRITE_ALLOW_EUC_PRESENTER_SELF: This rewrite allows calls from `self`
        (LOAS role of the process) to present EUCs. It covers typical in-
        process and loopback calls, as well as local ESF for One Platform
        APIs.
      REWRITE_DENY_EUC_PRESENTER_NON_BCID3_ROLES: This rewrite denies calls in
        which a non-BCID3 role is presenting forwarded EUCs. Instead, those
        non-BCID3 roles, such as humans and untrusted roles, are expected to
        use LOAS-based credentials only.
      REWRITE_DENY_EUC_PRESENTER_UNTRUSTED_PROXY_ROLES: This rewrite denies
        calls in which a untrusted proxy is presenting forwarded EUCs. The
        untrusted proxies include `untrusted-http-proxy` and `unauthenticated-
        esf-proxy` LOAS peer roles. These roles are "fake" LOAS peers, which
        indicate that the call was actually made over an unauthenticated
        channel. Per go/forwardable-creds, EUCs should only be presented over
        authenticated channels.
      REWRITE_DENY_PRIVILEGED_ACCESS_FORGE: This rewrite denies system access
        for Forge test roles. If you want to send RPCs from Forge, use a TEST
        policy.
      REWRITE_DENY_PRIVILEGED_ACCESS_RPCSTUDIO: This rewrite denies system
        access from the rpcStudio role (i.e., the `rpcstudio-client-initiated-
        rpc` role). The rpcStudio never makes requests on its own authority,
        so this rewrite will not interfere with any use of rpcStudio.
      REWRITE_ALLOW_EUC_PRESENTER_RPCSTUDIO: This rewrite allows the global
        rpcStudio instance to present EUCs, but does not grant it any access
        on its own authority. This rewrite is never enabled by default, and
        should only be manually enabled if you have read go/global-
        rpcstudio#server-setup, and intend to use the global rpcStudio
        instance.
      REWRITE_INSERT_DEFAULT_AUDIT_POLICY: This rewrite adds an audit config
        that contains an empty accessing process name (APN) when a policy
        specifies audit logging. If the APN is left empty, Gin can infer the
        APN in certain circumstances (go/annotation-only-aap).
      REWRITE_EXCLUDE_AI_AGENTS_FROM_PRIVILEGED_HUMAN_ACCESS: This rewrite
        excludes AI agents from being able to perform privileged access as
        human. AI agent accounts are treated as humans by RpcSP, subjecting
        them to admin access controls. This rewrite excludes AI agents from
        being able to perform privileged access as human, by adding a user
        attribution condition to the bindings that grant privileged access.
        This is currently only supported in RpcSP v2.
      REWRITE_ADD_AAC_CONDITION_AND_GIN_RULE: This rewrite adds an appropriate
        condition to the standard AAC DENY rule such that access by Googlers
        and AI agents are subject to admin access platform approval. It also
        adds a Gin LOG rule into the v1 policy. This is only applicable to
        RpcSP v1 policies.
    """
    REWRITE_UNSPECIFIED = 0
    REWRITE_ALLOW_EUC_PRESENTER_SELF = 1
    REWRITE_DENY_EUC_PRESENTER_NON_BCID3_ROLES = 2
    REWRITE_DENY_EUC_PRESENTER_UNTRUSTED_PROXY_ROLES = 3
    REWRITE_DENY_PRIVILEGED_ACCESS_FORGE = 4
    REWRITE_DENY_PRIVILEGED_ACCESS_RPCSTUDIO = 5
    REWRITE_ALLOW_EUC_PRESENTER_RPCSTUDIO = 6
    REWRITE_INSERT_DEFAULT_AUDIT_POLICY = 7
    REWRITE_EXCLUDE_AI_AGENTS_FROM_PRIVILEGED_HUMAN_ACCESS = 8
    REWRITE_ADD_AAC_CONDITION_AND_GIN_RULE = 9

