class RolloutThresholds(_messages.Message):
  r"""RolloutThresholds contains the thresholds used to determine the rollout
  state of a policy: disabled (not evaluated), evaluated in Audit-Only Mode
  (evaluated but not enforced), or hard enforced (evaluated and enforced).
  Note that at request time, the rollout state may be dictated by
  PrivacyContext signals (go/ptoken-dma-rollout#request-based-signals), in
  which case the thresholds are ignored. A request will be hard enforced if
  any of the following are true: - it falls within
  hard_enforcement_bucket_threshold - the request is considered online and
  falls within online_hard_enforcement_bucket_threshold - the request is
  considered offline and falls within
  offline_hard_enforcement_bucket_threshold It's recommended to choose between
  a single-threshold rollout (only set hard_enforcement_bucket_threshold) and
  a differentiated online/offline rollout (set both
  online_hard_enforcement_bucket_threshold and
  offline_hard_enforcement_bucket_threshold).) If the request is not hard
  enforced, random sampling using aom_percent will determine whether the
  request is evaluated in AOM mode (evaluated but not enforced). Otherwise,
  the policy will be disabled (not evaluated, not enforced). Hard enforcement
  thresholds range from [0, 110]. Each of these thresholds is compared against
  a corresponding rollout bucket in the range [1, 110]. Rollout buckets are
  encapsulated in the RolloutBucket class
  (privacy/ptoken/contrib/policy_rollouts/rollout_bucket.h) to ensure they are
  in the correct range. Buckets 1 - 10 are reserved for testing/dogfood, and
  currently are only used for online requests. If no RolloutBucket is provided
  or available via rollout_priority in the request's ComplianceControls, BRPR
  will autogenerate a RolloutBucket using the first available among: 1. the
  hashed gaia id of the request, (online requests only) 2. the hashed trace id
  of the request, 3. Use bucket 110 by default. These requests will launch
  last. If a random distribution is preferred in the case where no id is
  available, set the flag
  enable_random_fallback_for_hard_enforcement_rollout_bucket to true, and the
  RolloutBucket will be a random number in the range [11, 110]. LINT.IfChange

  Fields:
    aomPercent: To determine whether to run AOM on a request, a random
      sampling approach is used: the trace id is hashed and compared against
      the respective aom_percent. If a random distribtion is preferred in the
      case where the trace id is 0, set the flag
      enable_random_fallback_for_hard_enforcement_rollout_bucket to true.
      Applies to both online and offline traffic. Range [0, 100]
    hardEnforcementBucketThreshold: Hard enforcement threshold applied
      regardless of online/offline status. Use this field for a single-
      threshold rampup. Compared against the first available among: 1.
      RolloutOptions.bucket_for_hard_enforcement, if set 2. a bucket
      autogenerated by BRPR (see RolloutThresholds header comment) Range [0,
      110]
    offlineHardEnforcementBucketThreshold: Compared against the first
      available among: 1. RolloutOptions.bucket_for_hard_enforcement, if set
      2. a bucket autogenerated by BRPR (see RolloutThresholds header comment)
      If the request is offline and the RolloutBucket is <= this threshold,
      the request is hard enforced. Range [0, 110]
    onlineHardEnforcementBucketThreshold: Compared against a RolloutBucket
      autogenerated by BRPR using the first available among: 1. the
      rollout_priority in the request's ComplianceControls, 2. a bucket
      autogenerated by BRPR (see RolloutThresholds header comment) If the
      request is online and the RolloutBucket is <= this threshold, the
      request is hard enforced. Range [0, 110]
  """

  aomPercent = _messages.IntegerField(1, variant=_messages.Variant.INT32)
  hardEnforcementBucketThreshold = _messages.IntegerField(2, variant=_messages.Variant.INT32)
  offlineHardEnforcementBucketThreshold = _messages.IntegerField(3, variant=_messages.Variant.INT32)
  onlineHardEnforcementBucketThreshold = _messages.IntegerField(4, variant=_messages.Variant.INT32)


class RpcSecurityPolicy(_messages.Message):
  r"""Security policy determining how requests are authenticated and
  authorized.

  Enums:
    DisableChecksValueListEntryValuesEnum:
    DisableRewritesValueListEntryValuesEnum:
    EnableChecksValueListEntryValuesEnum:
    EnableRewritesValueListEntryValuesEnum:

  Fields:
    applicationPermissionPolicies: Role-based policies for associating
      application level `permissions` with `bindings` and `conditions`. Only
      for use with 2.0 `RpcSecurityPolicy`s, i.e. policies that contain
      `endpoint_policies`. These permissions can be checked with the
      LocalAuthorization library, see go/rpcsp-authz#rpcsp2 DO NOT USE: Under
      development, see b/327637894 for status.
    audit: Information on how to populate audit logs. Applies to all mappings
      and all endpoint_policies. See message documentation for more details.
      For RpcSP v1, this field is strongly recommended. For RpcSP v2, omit
      this field by default. When using standard Admin Access Protection
      (`admin_access_control`) or full logging (`ENABLE_LOGGING`), Gin
      allowlisting and auditing are managed automatically using your service's
      Borg role. Do NOT set this field explicitly for standard v2 policies;
      adding a manual audit clause is treated as an override that disables
      automatic allowlisting with Gin. You only need to set this field if you
      need to override the default APN, or if you opt out of standard AAP by
      using `disable_admin_access_control` with `ENABLE_LOGGING_LITE` (or
      leave `audit` unspecified inside it, which defaults to lite logging).
    disableChecks: Checks that are disabled in the RpcSP compiler
    disableRewrites: Rewrites that are disabled in the RpcSP compiler
    enableChecks: Checks that are enabled in the RpcSP compiler
    enableLabelEnforcement: SUPPORTED IN C++ ONLY. DO NOT USE THIS FIELD IN
      JAVA OR GO. If true, allows configuring label mappings in the policy and
      enforces them on requests. Label mappings are enforced for both Stubby
      and HTTP requests, and Stubby requests will prefer a matching
      `rpc_method` mapping over a matching `label` mapping. Label mappings are
      the supported means of enforcing RpcSP on HTTP endpoints and built-in
      RPC services (including ignored services governed by
      --rpc_security_policy_ignored_services). When this field is false, the
      policy will not be enforced on HTTP handlers and built-in RPC services.
      This field is not yet implemented in Java or Go and will cause a policy
      validation exception if set.
    enableRewrites: Rewrites that are enabled in the RpcSP compiler
    endpointPolicies: Simplified, role-based language for expressing
      RpcSecurityPolicy. Not yet available for some languages/platforms; see
      go/rpcsp2-features#status.
    legacyAllowLabelMappingsWithoutLabelEnforcement: DO NOT USE THIS FIELD. If
      true, allows services that have not enabled RpcSP label enforcement to
      specify label mappings in their policy. These mappings will not actually
      be enforced until the service migrates to RpcSP label enforcement. This
      field is a temporary measure used for migrating existing pilot HTTP
      enforcement users to RpcSP label enforcement. It should not be set in
      any policy that sets `enable_label_enforcement`. Setting this field has
      no effect in Java or Go, as pilot HTTP enforcement is C++ only.
    mapping: Authentication and (optionally) authorization policy, using a
      model consistent with Cloud Identity and Authorization Management
      (go/iam). See http://go/stubby-authz-2014#heading=h.nxwc3s5ss1ve for
      discussion of how credential policy and request authorization policy fit
      together in this model. For methods not matched by any mapping (see
      RpcSecurityPolicyMapping.rpc_method), the handling depends on the value
      of trust_esf_authentication field. If trust_esf_authentication is set to
      true and the request is ESF-authenticated, unmatched methods are treated
      as if MANUAL_IAM authorization mode applied (with empty
      permission_to_check). Otherwise, a default deny-all policy applies to
      all unmatched methods. Example: Service with a simple, coarse-grained
      authorization policy. mapping { rpc_method: '/WidgetService.Get'
      rpc_method: '/WidgetService.List' authentication_policy { creds_policy {
      # All clients can authenticate as themselves via LOAS. rules {
      permissions: 'auth.creds.useLOAS' action: ALLOW in: 'allUsers' } } }
      authorization_mode: AUTOMATIC_IAM # Authorization check in Stubby.
      permission_to_check: 'widgets.read' } mapping { rpc_method:
      '/WidgetService.Create' rpc_method: '/WidgetService.Update'
      authentication_policy { creds_policy { # All clients can authenticate as
      themselves via LOAS. rules { permissions: 'auth.creds.useLOAS' action:
      ALLOW in: 'allUsers' } } } authorization_mode: AUTOMATIC_IAM #
      Authorization check in Stubby. permission_to_check: 'widgets.write' } #
      Calls to any methods not listed above are rejected.
      system_authorization_policy { rules { permissions: 'widgets.read'
      action: ALLOW in: 'mdb:widget-clients-readonly' in: 'user:widget-
      indexer@prod.google.com' } rules { permissions: 'widgets.write'
      permissions: 'widgets.read' action: ALLOW in: 'mdb:widget-sre' in:
      'mdb:widget-clients-readwrite' } } Example: Service with coarse-grained
      privileged access policy and custom fine-grained authorization checks
      for end-user actions. mapping { rpc_method: '/Accounts.Create'
      rpc_method: '/Accounts.Get' rpc_method: '/Accounts.List' rpc_method:
      '/Accounts.Update' authentication_policy { creds_policy { # Frontend
      passes GaiaMints. rules { permissions: 'auth.creds.useNormalUserEUC'
      action: ALLOW in: 'user:accounts-frontend@prod.google.com' } # Admin
      frontend passes DATs or GaiaMints obtained on the authority # of a
      Googler. These may be "impersonation" credentials (subject # to the
      impersonation_policy below) or "SuperDATs". rules { permissions:
      'auth.creds.useProdUserEUC' action: ALLOW in: 'user:accounts-admin-
      frontend@prod.google.com' } # All clients can authenticate as themselves
      via LOAS, make # "anonymous" requests for public data, or (subject to
      the # impersonation_policy below) assert another user identity. rules {
      permissions: 'auth.creds.useLOAS' action: ALLOW in: 'allUsers' } }
      impersonation_policy { rules { permissions:
      'auth.impersonation.impersonateNormalUser' action: ALLOW # Tonic
      policies granting API_ACCOUNTS or LOAS-authenticated # clients who are
      authorized requesters under such a policy. in: 'mdb:tonic-scope-api-
      accounts' # This privileged client makes requests on the authority of #
      arbitrary users without providing creds for them. in: 'user:accounts-
      scheduled-task-executor@prod.google.com' } } } # Application uses
      Authorization::Check() to check for privileged # system-level access
      under the system_authorization_policy below. authorization_mode:
      MANUAL_IAM } # Calls to any methods not listed above are rejected.
      system_authorization_policy { # Generate audit logs for access by
      humans. rules { permissions: '*' action: LOG conditions { iam:
      ATTRIBUTION op: IN values: 'mdb:all-person-users' } log_config {
      data_access {} } } # System-level (privileged) access. rules {
      permissions: 'accounts.read' permissions: 'accounts.write' action: ALLOW
      in: 'mdb:accounts-sre' in: 'user:accounts-wipeout@prod.google.com' #
      Permits access using SuperDATs with scope API_ACCOUNTS. in: 'mdb:tonic-
      scope-api-accounts' } } Example: Legacy service with opaque (non-IAM),
      custom authorization checks. mapping { rpc_method: '/Doodads.*'
      authentication_policy { creds_policy { # Frontend passes GaiaMints.
      rules { in: 'accounts-frontend@prod.google.com' permission:
      'auth.creds.useNormalUserEUC' action: ALLOW } # All clients can
      authenticate as themselves via LOAS. rules { permissions:
      'auth.creds.useLOAS' action: ALLOW in: 'allUsers' } } } # Application
      uses custom code to check authorization against user() # in the
      SecurityContext. This field may contain an external Gaia # user (e.g.,
      in the NORMAL_USER_CREDS case) or an @prod.google.com # Gaia user (in
      the LOAS-authenticated or PROD_USER_CREDS case). authorization_mode =
      MANUAL_LEGACY } # Calls to any methods not listed above are rejected.
    methodPolicies: Per-method API policies. There's exactly one MethodPolicy
      generated for each method. Each MethodPolicy contains policy configs
      applicable to the method, including Cloud IAM policy, OrgPolicy, Cloud
      Audit Logging and Gin Logging. API policies defined here are enforced in
      addition to any matching RpcSecurityPolicyMapping. See go/cpe-
      integration-overview for integration guide.
    policyRequirement: Asserted policy requirement. This assertion is manually
      provided by policy producer and may be checked by presubmit checks or at
      process startup. When it is missing, startup enforcement checkpoint will
      be bypassed.
    rpcServices: RPC services referenced in the policy. If present, every RPC
      method listed in an EndpointPolicy or RpcSecurityPolicyMapping must
      correspond to a declared service. Only for use in go/rpcsp-central-repo
      policies.
    systemAuthorizationPolicy: Authorization policy granting broad ("system-
      level"), privileged access to selected internal users and groups. This
      policy will be consulted when checking 'permission_to_check' in
      AUTOMATIC_IAM authorization mode, and can also be used for custom
      authorization checks from application code (e.g., using
      LocalAuthorization::CheckMappedPermission()). See go/rpcsp/setup-
      guide.md#authz-policy.
    trustEsfAuthentication: If true, trust the authentication information
      supplied by ESF (go/esf) in deprecated ESF authentication modes,
      bypassing local authentication based on authentication_policy. This
      applies only to requests that ESF has authenticated and forwarded
      authentication results to the backend. Other requests, for example
      direct Stubby calls and calls forwarded via ESF in the recommended
      `use_rpc_security_policy: DELEGATED` mode, will still be subject to the
      authentication_policy check. DO NOT USE -- see http://go/api-auth#rpcsp
      for current guidance.
  """

  class DisableChecksValueListEntryValuesEnum(_messages.Enum):
    r"""DisableChecksValueListEntryValuesEnum enum type.

    Values:
      CHECK_UNSPECIFIED: <no description>
      CHECK_DENY_EUC_PRESENTER_ADMIN: This check complains if the
        `EUC_PRESENTER_ADMIN` role is used in a policy. Such role should not
        be used, as it allows humans to send EUCs, which prevents the server
        reaching RpcSecurityPolicy level 3.3.
      CHECK_REQUIRE_ORDER_INDEPENDENT_BINDINGS: This check ensures that
        bindings are used in a way that the ordering of the bindings isn't
        significant.
      CHECK_DENY_UNPRIVILEGED_USER_NARROW_BINDING: This check verifies that
        UNPRIVILEGED_USER does not grant access to a narrow set of users.
      CHECK_LANGUAGE_SUPPORT: This check verifies that a policy does not use
        unsupported language features. This is an internal compiler feature
        and cannot be disabled by users.
      CHECK_EXCLUDE_INSECURE_USERS_CONDITION: This check ensures that the
        `exclude_insecure_users` condition can only be used to restrict
        supported users. See go/rpcsp-levels#insecure-roles-list. This is an
        internal compiler feature and cannot be disabled by users.
      CHECK_ADMIN_ACCESS_CONTROLS: This check ensures that
        `admin_access_control` is only set on bindings for admin roles. This
        is an internal compiler feature and cannot be disabled by users.
      CHECK_CONFLICTING_GIN_LOGGING_BEHAVIORS: This check ensures that all
        *ADMIN roles (e.g. `ADMIN`, `EUC_PRESENTER_ADMIN`,
        `IMPERSONATOR_ADMIN`) for a single endpoint are using the same Gin
        logging failure behavior. Configuring two *ADMIN bindings where one
        binding is set to use fail closed Gin logging and the other is set to
        use fail open Gin logging is equivalent to configuring one binding
        that is using fail open Gin logging, and is therefore not allowed.
      CHECK_NO_HTTP_HANDLERS_FIELD: This check complains if an
        `endpoint_policy` uses the experimental `http_handlers` field.
      CHECK_NO_OAUTH_IN_BINDINGS: This check ensures `OauthCondition` is NOT
        used at the `Binding`-level, only at the `EndpointPolicy`-level.
      CHECK_CORP_GROUPS_HTTPSP_ONLY: This check limits the use of the field
        `corp_groups` to HttpSP.
      CHECK_REQUIRE_DG_ANNOTATIONS: This check requires that all `mapping` and
        `endpoint_policies` entries configure Data Governance Annotations
        (go/dga). This check is never enabled by default, and should only be
        enabled if you understand its effects.
      CHECK_CORP_GROUPS_ALLOWED_BINDINGS_ONLY: This check limits the use of
        the field `corp_groups` to only `ADMIN` and `SYSTEM` bindings.
      CHECK_NO_LOCAL_ACLS: This check prohibits use of go/localacl (RpcSP2
        `local_prod_groups` and RpcSP1 `local:groupname`). Uses of this
        feature must be approved by ProdSec Labs.
      CHECK_NO_STUBBY_APPLICATION_AUTHORIZATION: This check prevents a mapping
        or endpoint_policy from allowing [fine-grained
        authorization](http://go/rpc-security-simple#authorization). If a
        service records fine-grained authorization results, then this check
        can be safely disabled. Warning: framework-specific filtering is
        applied for this check in [PolicyChecker::Check](http://cs/function:rp
        csp::internal::PolicyChecker::Check$). Currently, only applicable to
        Stubby services.
      CHECK_HTTPSP_AUDIT_ONLY_ENABLE_OR_DISABLE: This check prevents the use
        of values except `ENABLE_LOGGING` or `DISABLE_LOGGING` for `audit` in
        `disabled_admin_access_controls` for an HttpSP policy. HttpSP Gin
        logging has a hard dependency on AAP because that is where auto
        population of the DataAccessLog based on the incoming HTTP request
        occurs. As a result, we disallow Gin logging without consultation of
        AAP for HttpSP, i.e. `ENABLE_LOGGING_LITE`.
      CHECK_NO_EXCLUDE_PEER_USER_CONDITION: This check prevents use of the
        `exclude_peer_user` and `exclude_primary_peer_user` conditions.
      CHECK_REQUIRE_RPC_SERVICES_FIELD: This check enforces the existence of
        the `rpc_services` field. Warning: framework-specific filtering is
        applied for this check in [PolicyChecker::Check](http://cs/function:rp
        csp::internal::PolicyChecker::Check$). Currently, only applicable to
        Stubby services.
      CHECK_HTTPSP_MEETING_CORP_WEB_MANDATORY_CONTROL: This check requires the
        HttpSP policy to meet the requirements of the mandatory control for
        corp web access: 1. The policy is from an EASE managed directory (e.g.
        google3/configs/security/rpcsp/teams/aclaim/managed/). 2. The policy
        allows only devices of fully trusted tier and LOAS origins. 3. Gin
        logging is enabled. 4. Privileged access is subject to AAP control. 5.
        The policy is HttpSP policy (i.e., endpoints are identified using only
        `http_handlers` or `http_paths`). 6. The policy is in PROD
        environment. This check is never enabled by default, and should only
        be enabled if you understand its effects. See go/rpcsp2-policy-
        controls#check-httpsp-meeting-corp-web-mandatory-control
      CHECK_NO_OVERLAPPING_HUMAN_PROD_BINDINGS: This check enforces that no
        more than one binding may grant any given permission to the same set
        of human prod users. If this check passes, it means that binding order
        (and the major exception documented at go/rpcsp2-binding-
        evaluation#binding-evaluation-order) cannot affect how requests from
        human prod users are evaluated. This check is intentionally very
        strict, and serves as a guardrail to encourage more readable policies.
        It may be disabled without an exemption, but we strongly recommend
        that service owners only do so if the check is failing due to a false
        positive, or if you require multiple overlapping admin bindings and
        are following the guidelines at go/rpcsp2-configuring-admin-role.
      CHECK_NO_ALL_NORMAL_USERS_IN_SYSTEM_ROLE: This check prevents the use of
        `all_normal_users` in bindings for the `SYSTEM` role. **Why this check
        exists:** The `SYSTEM` role only grants access to non-person prod
        users (such as Borg roles, see go/rpcsp2-roles#system-vs-admin-roles),
        but `all_normal_users` matches authenticated Gaia users who are *not*
        prod users. Therefore, granting the `SYSTEM` role to
        `all_normal_users` has no effect; this check prevents users from
        configuring RpcSP in this confusing way. **How to fix it:** If your
        policy fails this check, you must remove `all_normal_users` from the
        `members` field of any binding where `role` is `SYSTEM`. Replace it
        with a more specific set of users or groups that require this level of
        access.
    """
    CHECK_UNSPECIFIED = 0
    CHECK_DENY_EUC_PRESENTER_ADMIN = 1
    CHECK_REQUIRE_ORDER_INDEPENDENT_BINDINGS = 2
    CHECK_DENY_UNPRIVILEGED_USER_NARROW_BINDING = 3
    CHECK_LANGUAGE_SUPPORT = 4
    CHECK_EXCLUDE_INSECURE_USERS_CONDITION = 5
    CHECK_ADMIN_ACCESS_CONTROLS = 6
    CHECK_CONFLICTING_GIN_LOGGING_BEHAVIORS = 7
    CHECK_NO_HTTP_HANDLERS_FIELD = 8
    CHECK_NO_OAUTH_IN_BINDINGS = 9
    CHECK_CORP_GROUPS_HTTPSP_ONLY = 10
    CHECK_REQUIRE_DG_ANNOTATIONS = 11
    CHECK_CORP_GROUPS_ALLOWED_BINDINGS_ONLY = 12
    CHECK_NO_LOCAL_ACLS = 13
    CHECK_NO_STUBBY_APPLICATION_AUTHORIZATION = 14
    CHECK_HTTPSP_AUDIT_ONLY_ENABLE_OR_DISABLE = 15
    CHECK_NO_EXCLUDE_PEER_USER_CONDITION = 16
    CHECK_REQUIRE_RPC_SERVICES_FIELD = 17
    CHECK_HTTPSP_MEETING_CORP_WEB_MANDATORY_CONTROL = 18
    CHECK_NO_OVERLAPPING_HUMAN_PROD_BINDINGS = 19
    CHECK_NO_ALL_NORMAL_USERS_IN_SYSTEM_ROLE = 20

  class DisableRewritesValueListEntryValuesEnum(_messages.Enum):
    r"""DisableRewritesValueListEntryValuesEnum enum type.

    Values:
      REWRITE_UNSPECIFIED: <no description>
      REWRITE_ALLOW_EUC_PRESENTER_SELF: This rewrite allows calls from `self`
        (LOAS role of the process) to present EUCs. It covers typical in-
        process and loopback calls, as well as local ESF for One Platform
        APIs.
      REWRITE_DENY_EUC_PRESENTER_NON_BCID3_ROLES: This rewrite denies calls in
        which a non-BCID3 role is presenting forwarded EUCs. Instead, those
        non-BCID3 roles, such as humans and untrusted roles, are expected to
        use LOAS-based credentials only.
      REWRITE_DENY_EUC_PRESENTER_UNTRUSTED_PROXY_ROLES: This rewrite denies
        calls in which a untrusted proxy is presenting forwarded EUCs. The
        untrusted proxies include `untrusted-http-proxy` and `unauthenticated-
        esf-proxy` LOAS peer roles. These roles are "fake" LOAS peers, which
        indicate that the call was actually made over an unauthenticated
        channel. Per go/forwardable-creds, EUCs should only be presented over
        authenticated channels.
      REWRITE_DENY_PRIVILEGED_ACCESS_FORGE: This rewrite denies system access
        for Forge test roles. If you want to send RPCs from Forge, use a TEST
        policy.
      REWRITE_DENY_PRIVILEGED_ACCESS_RPCSTUDIO: This rewrite denies system
        access from the rpcStudio role (i.e., the `rpcstudio-client-initiated-
        rpc` role). The rpcStudio never makes requests on its own authority,
        so this rewrite will not interfere with any use of rpcStudio.
      REWRITE_ALLOW_EUC_PRESENTER_RPCSTUDIO: This rewrite allows the global
        rpcStudio instance to present EUCs, but does not grant it any access
        on its own authority. This rewrite is never enabled by default, and
        should only be manually enabled if you have read go/global-
        rpcstudio#server-setup, and intend to use the global rpcStudio
        instance.
      REWRITE_INSERT_DEFAULT_AUDIT_POLICY: This rewrite adds an audit config
        that contains an empty accessing process name (APN) when a policy
        specifies audit logging. If the APN is left empty, Gin can infer the
        APN in certain circumstances (go/annotation-only-aap).
      REWRITE_EXCLUDE_AI_AGENTS_FROM_PRIVILEGED_HUMAN_ACCESS: This rewrite
        excludes AI agents from being able to perform privileged access as
        human. AI agent accounts are treated as humans by RpcSP, subjecting
        them to admin access controls. This rewrite excludes AI agents from
        being able to perform privileged access as human, by adding a user
        attribution condition to the bindings that grant privileged access.
        This is currently only supported in RpcSP v2.
      REWRITE_ADD_AAC_CONDITION_AND_GIN_RULE: This rewrite adds an appropriate
        condition to the standard AAC DENY rule such that access by Googlers
        and AI agents are subject to admin access platform approval. It also
        adds a Gin LOG rule into the v1 policy. This is only applicable to
        RpcSP v1 policies.
    """
    REWRITE_UNSPECIFIED = 0
    REWRITE_ALLOW_EUC_PRESENTER_SELF = 1
    REWRITE_DENY_EUC_PRESENTER_NON_BCID3_ROLES = 2
    REWRITE_DENY_EUC_PRESENTER_UNTRUSTED_PROXY_ROLES = 3
    REWRITE_DENY_PRIVILEGED_ACCESS_FORGE = 4
    REWRITE_DENY_PRIVILEGED_ACCESS_RPCSTUDIO = 5
    REWRITE_ALLOW_EUC_PRESENTER_RPCSTUDIO = 6
    REWRITE_INSERT_DEFAULT_AUDIT_POLICY = 7
    REWRITE_EXCLUDE_AI_AGENTS_FROM_PRIVILEGED_HUMAN_ACCESS = 8
    REWRITE_ADD_AAC_CONDITION_AND_GIN_RULE = 9

  class EnableChecksValueListEntryValuesEnum(_messages.Enum):
    r"""EnableChecksValueListEntryValuesEnum enum type.

    Values:
      CHECK_UNSPECIFIED: <no description>
      CHECK_DENY_EUC_PRESENTER_ADMIN: This check complains if the
        `EUC_PRESENTER_ADMIN` role is used in a policy. Such role should not
        be used, as it allows humans to send EUCs, which prevents the server
        reaching RpcSecurityPolicy level 3.3.
      CHECK_REQUIRE_ORDER_INDEPENDENT_BINDINGS: This check ensures that
        bindings are used in a way that the ordering of the bindings isn't
        significant.
      CHECK_DENY_UNPRIVILEGED_USER_NARROW_BINDING: This check verifies that
        UNPRIVILEGED_USER does not grant access to a narrow set of users.
      CHECK_LANGUAGE_SUPPORT: This check verifies that a policy does not use
        unsupported language features. This is an internal compiler feature
        and cannot be disabled by users.
      CHECK_EXCLUDE_INSECURE_USERS_CONDITION: This check ensures that the
        `exclude_insecure_users` condition can only be used to restrict
        supported users. See go/rpcsp-levels#insecure-roles-list. This is an
        internal compiler feature and cannot be disabled by users.
      CHECK_ADMIN_ACCESS_CONTROLS: This check ensures that
        `admin_access_control` is only set on bindings for admin roles. This
        is an internal compiler feature and cannot be disabled by users.
      CHECK_CONFLICTING_GIN_LOGGING_BEHAVIORS: This check ensures that all
        *ADMIN roles (e.g. `ADMIN`, `EUC_PRESENTER_ADMIN`,
        `IMPERSONATOR_ADMIN`) for a single endpoint are using the same Gin
        logging failure behavior. Configuring two *ADMIN bindings where one
        binding is set to use fail closed Gin logging and the other is set to
        use fail open Gin logging is equivalent to configuring one binding
        that is using fail open Gin logging, and is therefore not allowed.
      CHECK_NO_HTTP_HANDLERS_FIELD: This check complains if an
        `endpoint_policy` uses the experimental `http_handlers` field.
      CHECK_NO_OAUTH_IN_BINDINGS: This check ensures `OauthCondition` is NOT
        used at the `Binding`-level, only at the `EndpointPolicy`-level.
      CHECK_CORP_GROUPS_HTTPSP_ONLY: This check limits the use of the field
        `corp_groups` to HttpSP.
      CHECK_REQUIRE_DG_ANNOTATIONS: This check requires that all `mapping` and
        `endpoint_policies` entries configure Data Governance Annotations
        (go/dga). This check is never enabled by default, and should only be
        enabled if you understand its effects.
      CHECK_CORP_GROUPS_ALLOWED_BINDINGS_ONLY: This check limits the use of
        the field `corp_groups` to only `ADMIN` and `SYSTEM` bindings.
      CHECK_NO_LOCAL_ACLS: This check prohibits use of go/localacl (RpcSP2
        `local_prod_groups` and RpcSP1 `local:groupname`). Uses of this
        feature must be approved by ProdSec Labs.
      CHECK_NO_STUBBY_APPLICATION_AUTHORIZATION: This check prevents a mapping
        or endpoint_policy from allowing [fine-grained
        authorization](http://go/rpc-security-simple#authorization). If a
        service records fine-grained authorization results, then this check
        can be safely disabled. Warning: framework-specific filtering is
        applied for this check in [PolicyChecker::Check](http://cs/function:rp
        csp::internal::PolicyChecker::Check$). Currently, only applicable to
        Stubby services.
      CHECK_HTTPSP_AUDIT_ONLY_ENABLE_OR_DISABLE: This check prevents the use
        of values except `ENABLE_LOGGING` or `DISABLE_LOGGING` for `audit` in
        `disabled_admin_access_controls` for an HttpSP policy. HttpSP Gin
        logging has a hard dependency on AAP because that is where auto
        population of the DataAccessLog based on the incoming HTTP request
        occurs. As a result, we disallow Gin logging without consultation of
        AAP for HttpSP, i.e. `ENABLE_LOGGING_LITE`.
      CHECK_NO_EXCLUDE_PEER_USER_CONDITION: This check prevents use of the
        `exclude_peer_user` and `exclude_primary_peer_user` conditions.
      CHECK_REQUIRE_RPC_SERVICES_FIELD: This check enforces the existence of
        the `rpc_services` field. Warning: framework-specific filtering is
        applied for this check in [PolicyChecker::Check](http://cs/function:rp
        csp::internal::PolicyChecker::Check$). Currently, only applicable to
        Stubby services.
      CHECK_HTTPSP_MEETING_CORP_WEB_MANDATORY_CONTROL: This check requires the
        HttpSP policy to meet the requirements of the mandatory control for
        corp web access: 1. The policy is from an EASE managed directory (e.g.
        google3/configs/security/rpcsp/teams/aclaim/managed/). 2. The policy
        allows only devices of fully trusted tier and LOAS origins. 3. Gin
        logging is enabled. 4. Privileged access is subject to AAP control. 5.
        The policy is HttpSP policy (i.e., endpoints are identified using only
        `http_handlers` or `http_paths`). 6. The policy is in PROD
        environment. This check is never enabled by default, and should only
        be enabled if you understand its effects. See go/rpcsp2-policy-
        controls#check-httpsp-meeting-corp-web-mandatory-control
      CHECK_NO_OVERLAPPING_HUMAN_PROD_BINDINGS: This check enforces that no
        more than one binding may grant any given permission to the same set
        of human prod users. If this check passes, it means that binding order
        (and the major exception documented at go/rpcsp2-binding-
        evaluation#binding-evaluation-order) cannot affect how requests from
        human prod users are evaluated. This check is intentionally very
        strict, and serves as a guardrail to encourage more readable policies.
        It may be disabled without an exemption, but we strongly recommend
        that service owners only do so if the check is failing due to a false
        positive, or if you require multiple overlapping admin bindings and
        are following the guidelines at go/rpcsp2-configuring-admin-role.
      CHECK_NO_ALL_NORMAL_USERS_IN_SYSTEM_ROLE: This check prevents the use of
        `all_normal_users` in bindings for the `SYSTEM` role. **Why this check
        exists:** The `SYSTEM` role only grants access to non-person prod
        users (such as Borg roles, see go/rpcsp2-roles#system-vs-admin-roles),
        but `all_normal_users` matches authenticated Gaia users who are *not*
        prod users. Therefore, granting the `SYSTEM` role to
        `all_normal_users` has no effect; this check prevents users from
        configuring RpcSP in this confusing way. **How to fix it:** If your
        policy fails this check, you must remove `all_normal_users` from the
        `members` field of any binding where `role` is `SYSTEM`. Replace it
        with a more specific set of users or groups that require this level of
        access.
    """
    CHECK_UNSPECIFIED = 0
    CHECK_DENY_EUC_PRESENTER_ADMIN = 1
    CHECK_REQUIRE_ORDER_INDEPENDENT_BINDINGS = 2
    CHECK_DENY_UNPRIVILEGED_USER_NARROW_BINDING = 3
    CHECK_LANGUAGE_SUPPORT = 4
